---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { FARE_TABLE } from "../lib/fixtures";

const fareSummary = `$${FARE_TABLE.adult.offPeak.toFixed(2)} off-peak to $${FARE_TABLE.adult.peak.toFixed(2)} at peak (concession from $${FARE_TABLE.concession.offPeak.toFixed(2)} to $${FARE_TABLE.concession.peak.toFixed(2)})`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Home">
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>The MyWay+ account, redesigned</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Transport Canberra's MyWay+ replaced the old MyWay card in late 2024,
      promising simpler ticketing, real-time journey planning and more ways
      to pay. This is a concept redesign of the part riders actually deal
      with every week — the account portal — built around what the launch
      didn't deliver.
    </p>
    <a class="cta" href="./dashboard/">See the redesigned dashboard →</a>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why this needed a rebuild</h2>
    <p>
      MyWay+ went live on 27 November 2024 after Transport Canberra's own
      review predicted a "sub-optimal but functional" result. Validators
      failed at major interchanges on day one, an overcharging incident
      forced NEC to audit every transaction since launch, and a Legislative
      Assembly inquiry later found the system "clearly not ready for
      launch." Independent reporting still described it as broken more than
      a year on.
    </p>
    <p>
      The account app carries its own share of the problem. Its most-cited
      complaint, echoed across hundreds of reviews, isn't a missing feature
      — it's that "account management" just opens an embedded browser
      pointed at the same web portal, instead of showing your balance, QR
      pass or trip history natively. This redesign starts from that specific
      complaint: a dashboard, top-up, trip history and settings that are
      part of the app, not a webview bolted onto it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the real system is meant to do</h2>
    <p>
      Transport Canberra frames MyWay+ as a step toward accessible,
      flexible, reliable and sustainable transport for Canberra — ticketing
      simple enough that it gets more people onto buses and light rail
      instead of driving. That's a mission worth taking seriously even where
      the execution hasn't matched it, so this redesign keeps the real fares
      and rebuilds the interface around them: adult fares run from{" "}
      {fareSummary}, current as of {FARE_TABLE.effectiveFrom}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="disclosure">
    <h2>About this prototype</h2>
    <p>
      This is a static, non-functional demo built for a university course —
      not an official Transport Canberra or ACT Government product. Signing
      in doesn't check a real password, and the balance, trip history and
      concession status you'll see are invented sample data to demonstrate
      the interface. The fares, concession categories and contact
      information are the real, current MyWay+ ones, restated in plain
      language rather than copied from the source.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-7);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-brand);
    color: var(--color-brand-contrast);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-5);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .cta:hover {
    background: var(--color-brand-strong);
    color: var(--color-brand-contrast);
  }

  section {
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    border-top: var(--border-width) solid var(--color-border);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  section h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  section p {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .disclosure {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
